<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan de Lanuza y Urrea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Juan de Lanuza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan de Lanuza y Urrea was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-of-aragon">Justicia of Aragon</a>, the judge charged with upholding the kingdom's fueros against royal power. When Philip II tried to seize his fugitive secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-perez">Antonio Pérez</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1591, Lanuza called the Aragonese to resist; a royal army entered the city, and he was beheaded without trial in December 1591. He became a symbol of Aragonese liberties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-lanuza-y-urrea.jpg" alt="Juan de Lanuza y Urrea" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo López del Plano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Last_moments_of_Juan_de_Lanuza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan de Lanuza y Urrea, called the Younger, was born in 1564, the son of Juan de Lanuza y Perellós, himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-of-aragon">Justicia of Aragon</a>, and Catalina Jiménez de Urrea. When his father died on 22 September 1591 he succeeded him in an office that was in practice hereditary in the family; he would be the last of his line to hold it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He took office in the midst of the Alterations of Aragon, which had begun in April 1590 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-perez">Antonio Pérez</a> sought protection from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> by invoking the Privileges of Aragon. The king circumvented them by accusing Pérez of heresy, which placed him under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>. Two days after taking office Lanuza was to hand Pérez over, but during his transfer to the Inquisition's prison a riot of nobles, clergy and common people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> set him free.</p><p>When the king's troops crossed the border and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, Lanuza declared their entry contrary to the Fueros of Aragon. The deputies of Aragon appealed to Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> without success, and most Aragonese towns first waited and then distanced themselves from Zaragoza. Quarrels divided the nobles leading the revolt and several fled. Lanuza marched out at the head of poorly trained men, who soon scattered; the royal army entered the city a few days later unopposed and arrested him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was beheaded without trial on 20 December 1591. His execution was a turning point in the history of Aragon: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Tarazona</a> in 1592 scaled back its medieval rights, and central royal authority and the Inquisition grew stronger.</p><p>In 1904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> raised a monument in the Plaza de Aragón to the Justiciazgo and the rights and freedoms of Aragon. A plaque in the city's Central Market, where he was executed, was placed on 20 December 1991, the 400th anniversary of his death, and since 1985 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-aragon">Cortes of Aragon</a> have kept 20 December as the Day of the Rights and Freedoms of the Aragonese People.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan de Lanuza y Urrea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tensions surfaced in 1591, when Philip II sent an army into Zaragoza over the affair of his fugitive secretary Antonio Pérez and executed the Justicia, <b>Juan de Lanuza</b>, and in the Catalan revolt of 1640–1652.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1591 the crown's attempt to remove Philip II's fugitive secretary Antonio Pérez from the Justicia's prison provoked riots in Zaragoza; a royal army entered the kingdom, the Justicia <b>Juan de Lanuza</b> was executed without trial, and the Cortes of Tarazona in 1592 curtailed the fueros.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-0.88&amp;year=1581" aria-label="Zaragoza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juan de Lanuza y Urrea"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4944 4010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4944 4010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-0.88&amp;year=1581">What was here in 1581? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
